\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $G=(V,E)$ be a connected locally finite planar graph. In independent site percolation with parameter $p$, each vertex is open with probability $p$, independently of all others. An open cluster is a connected component of the subgraph induced by open vertices. Write $N_\infty$ for the number of infinite open clusters and $\mathbb P_p$ for this product probability law.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
At $p=1/2$, does
\[\mathbb P_{1/2}(N_\infty\ge1)=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\mathbb P_{1/2}(N_\infty=\infty)=1\]
hold for every such planar graph? This is the site-percolation question, with no transitivity or minimum-degree hypothesis. \href{https://emis.de/ft/41630}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does almost-sure site percolation at probability one half on a planar graph force infinitely many infinite open clusters?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 10000087 (AMR-099-0087). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] I. Benjamini and O. Schramm, \emph{Percolation beyond $\mathbb Z^d$, many questions and a few answers}, Electronic Communications in Probability 1 (1996), pp. 71--82, Conjecture 8, p. 80, with the conventions of Section 2. \url{https://emis.de/ft/41630}.
\end{description}
